CertHub.register({"id":"securityx","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA SecurityX","short":"SecurityX","exam":"CAS-005","blurb":"Advanced, expert-level security certification (formerly CASP+) for senior practitioners who design, engineer and run enterprise security: governance and risk, security architecture, security engineering and security operations.","status":"check","statusNote":"The four domains and weights (Governance, risk and compliance 20%; Security architecture 27%; Security engineering 31%; Security operations 22%) come from CompTIA's published CAS-005 exam objectives as best known on Sept 25, 2026. comptia.org could not be opened from this environment, so confirm the domains, weights, exam length and objective numbering on the official SecurityX page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Maximum of 90 (multiple choice and performance-based)","minutes":165,"pass":"Pass/fail only (no scaled score is reported)","extra":"CompTIA recommends about ten years of IT experience, including five years of hands-on security work. SecurityX replaced CASP+ (CAS-004); existing CASP+ holders were carried over to SecurityX. Certification is renewed every three years through continuing education."},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":165},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA SecurityX certification page (exam details and objectives download)","url":"https://www.comptia.org/certifications/securityx"}],"planWeeks":14,"hoursPerWeek":"8–10","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Governance, risk and compliance","w":20,"topics":["Security governance components: policies, standards, procedures, guidelines and governance frameworks","Security program management: roles and responsibilities (RACI), awareness training, metrics and reporting to leadership","Change, configuration and asset management governance, including CMDB and data inventory","Risk management activities: impact analysis, risk assessment, risk appetite and tolerance, risk treatment and risk registers","Third-party and supply chain risk management: vendor assessments, SBOMs, contracts and right to audit","Business continuity and disaster recovery planning: BIA, RTO, RPO and plan testing","Compliance and regulatory impacts: GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS, SOX, data sovereignty and industry frameworks","Security frameworks and standards: NIST CSF, NIST SP 800-53, ISO/IEC 27001, CIS Controls and CSA CCM","Legal and privacy considerations: data subject rights, breach notification, e-discovery and legal holds","Threat modeling methods: STRIDE, PASTA, attack trees, MITRE ATT&CK and attack surface analysis","AI adoption challenges: AI governance, data privacy, prompt injection, model poisoning and acceptable use"],"notes":["Domain 1 objectives: governance components, risk management activities, compliance and regulatory impacts, threat modeling, and challenges of adopting AI. Confirm objective numbering in the official CAS-005 objectives."],"labs":["Build a risk register in a free spreadsheet (Google Sheets or LibreOffice Calc) for a fictional clinic: list 10 risks with likelihood, impact, inherent score, owner, treatment choice and residual score, then write a one-paragraph risk appetite statement and flag any risk above it.","Download the free Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool (Windows) or use OWASP Threat Dragon (free, runs in the browser or desktop), model a three-tier web app with a login flow, and record at least one STRIDE threat and one mitigation per element.","Use the free NIST CSF 2.0 reference materials to map five controls from your organization or a fictional one to CSF functions (Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), and note which ones would satisfy PCI DSS or HIPAA requirements as well."]},{"id":2,"name":"Security architecture","w":27,"topics":["Resilient system design: high availability, redundancy, load balancing, geographic dispersion and graceful degradation","Secure network architecture: segmentation, microsegmentation, screened subnets, NAC and software-defined networking","Zero trust architecture: policy decision and enforcement points, continuous verification and least privilege","Security in the software development life cycle: requirements, secure design reviews, SAST, DAST, SCA and CI/CD pipeline security","Integrating security controls and troubleshooting: firewalls, WAF, proxies, IDS/IPS, SIEM and log collection","Data security architecture: classification, labeling, DLP, data lifecycle, tokenization and masking","Identity and access architecture: federation (SAML, OIDC, OAuth 2.0), SSO, conditional access and privileged access management","Cloud security architecture: shared responsibility, CASB, SASE, cloud workload protection and CSPM","Container and serverless security: image scanning, orchestration hardening, secrets management and API gateways","Hybrid and multicloud design: connectivity, key management, consistent policy and cloud-to-on-premises integration","Secure architecture for remote access and collaboration: VPN, ZTNA, VDI and secure email gateways"],"notes":["Domain 2 objectives: designing resilient systems, integrating security into the SDLC, integrating and troubleshooting security controls, data security, identity and access, cloud and zero trust architecture. Confirm objective numbering in the official CAS-005 objectives."],"labs":["Free AWS, Azure or Google Cloud account: create a VPC/VNet with a public and a private subnet, put a security group or NSG on each, and prove that only the public tier can reach the private tier on one port. Stay in the free tier (no NAT gateways or paid load balancers) and delete everything when done.","Install the free Trivy scanner and scan a public container image (for example an old nginx tag) and a sample Dockerfile; list the critical findings, pick a smaller base image and rescan to show the difference.","Run a free SAST scan with Semgrep (community rules) against a deliberately vulnerable practice app such as OWASP Juice Shop source on your own machine, then triage three findings as true or false positives and write the fix you would require before merge."]},{"id":3,"name":"Security engineering","w":31,"topics":["Troubleshooting IAM: authentication failures, federation trust issues, certificate-based auth and MFA problems","Endpoint and server hardening: secure baselines, application allow lists, EDR, host firewalls and patching","Hardware security: TPM, HSM, secure boot, measured boot, secure enclaves and firmware integrity","Specialized and legacy systems: OT/ICS/SCADA, IoT, embedded systems and compensating controls","Security automation: scripting (PowerShell, Python, Bash), SOAR playbooks, infrastructure as code and configuration drift","Advanced cryptographic concepts: post-quantum cryptography, key stretching, forward secrecy, homomorphic encryption and envelope encryption","Cryptographic use cases: data at rest, in transit and in use, code signing, digital signatures and secure key exchange","PKI engineering: certificate lifecycle, CA hierarchy, OCSP and CRLs, certificate pinning and mutual TLS","Email and DNS security engineering: SPF, DKIM, DMARC, DNSSEC and S/MIME","Mobile and endpoint management: MDM/UEM, containerization, device attestation and BYOD controls","Secrets and key management: vaults, key rotation, KMS, hardware-backed keys and separation of duties","Secure configuration of network infrastructure: SNMPv3, SSH, management plane protection and secure routing"],"notes":["Domain 3 objectives: troubleshooting IAM, hardening endpoints and servers, hardware security, specialized and legacy systems, automation, and advanced cryptography and its use cases. Confirm objective numbering in the official CAS-005 objectives."],"labs":["On a free Linux VM (VirtualBox or a free-tier cloud instance), run the free Lynis audit, fix five hardening findings (SSH key-only login, firewall with ufw or firewalld, unused services removed, automatic updates, auditd), and rerun to compare the hardening index. Delete any cloud instance when finished.","Build a two-tier PKI with OpenSSL on your own machine: create an offline root CA, an intermediate CA and a server certificate with a SAN, then verify the chain with `openssl verify -CAfile` and inspect it with `openssl x509 -text`.","Look up SPF, DKIM and DMARC records for three public domains with `dig TXT` (for example `dig TXT _dmarc.example.com`), explain each policy (p=none, quarantine, reject), and write the DMARC record you would roll out in stages for your own domain."]},{"id":4,"name":"Security operations","w":22,"topics":["Monitoring and response data: SIEM correlation, log aggregation, event parsing, baselines and alert tuning","Threat intelligence: sources, STIX/TAXII, indicators of compromise, TTPs and intelligence sharing","Threat hunting: hypothesis-driven hunts, behavioral analytics, UEBA and hunting in endpoint telemetry","Vulnerability management: scanning, CVSS and EPSS prioritization, false positives and remediation tracking","Analyzing vulnerabilities and attacks: injection, deserialization, race conditions, memory safety and misconfigurations","Malware and indicator analysis: static vs dynamic analysis, sandboxing, YARA rules and file hashing","Incident response process: preparation, detection, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned","Digital forensics: order of volatility, chain of custody, memory and disk acquisition, and timeline analysis","Attack surface management and exposure reduction: asset discovery, external scanning and penetration test findings","Detection engineering: writing and testing detection rules, Sigma, MITRE ATT&CK coverage mapping and deception technologies"],"notes":["Domain 4 objectives: using monitoring and response data, analyzing vulnerabilities and attacks, threat hunting with intelligence, and analyzing data and artifacts in incident response. Confirm objective numbering in the official CAS-005 objectives."],"labs":["Install the free Security Onion or a Splunk Free / Elastic trial on a local VM, ingest a public sample dataset (such as the Splunk Boss of the SOC data or Security Onion's sample PCAPs), and build one correlation search that finds repeated failed logins followed by a success.","Write a YARA rule that matches a harmless test file you create (for example a text file containing a unique marker string), run it with the free `yara` CLI across a folder, then add a hash-based condition and explain the trade-offs.","Use the free MITRE ATT&CK Navigator in your browser to color the techniques your current (or a fictional) SOC can detect, pick three gaps, and write a Sigma rule idea and data source for each."]}],"study":{"1":[["How do policies, standards, procedures and guidelines differ?","A policy is a high-level, mandatory statement of intent approved by leadership. A standard makes it measurable (for example AES-256 for data at rest). A procedure is the step-by-step way to do a task. A guideline is recommended but optional advice."],["Explain inherent vs residual risk and how risk appetite is used.","Inherent risk is the level before controls; residual risk is what remains after controls. Leadership sets risk appetite (how much risk it will accept in pursuit of goals) and tolerance (acceptable variation); residual risks above it must be treated further or formally accepted by an owner with authority."],["Name the four risk treatment options with an example of each.","Mitigate (add MFA to reduce account takeover), transfer (buy cyber insurance or outsource with contractual liability), avoid (stop offering a risky service), accept (document and sign off on a low risk that costs more to fix than it could lose)."],["What should a third-party risk program include?","Tiering vendors by the data and access they have, due-diligence questionnaires and evidence (SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001), contract clauses (right to audit, breach notification, data handling, subprocessors), SBOMs for software, and ongoing monitoring and offboarding."],["How do STRIDE and MITRE ATT&CK differ in threat modeling?","STRIDE is a design-time category checklist (spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, elevation of privilege) applied to each element of a data flow diagram. ATT&CK is a knowledge base of observed adversary tactics and techniques, used to reason about real attacker behavior and detection coverage."],["What governance should an organization put in place before adopting generative AI?","An AI acceptable use policy, an inventory of approved tools, data classification rules for what may be entered, vendor terms on data retention and training, human review of outputs, and technical controls against prompt injection, data leakage and model poisoning."]],"2":[["What are the core components of a zero trust architecture?","A policy engine and policy administrator (together the policy decision point) that evaluate identity, device posture and context for every request, and policy enforcement points in front of resources. Access is least privilege, per session and continuously re-evaluated, with no implicit trust from network location."],["Where do SAST, DAST, SCA and IAST fit in the SDLC?","SAST analyzes source code early (IDE, pull request). SCA inventories third-party libraries and flags known vulnerabilities and licenses. DAST tests a running app from the outside in test or staging. IAST instruments the running app during testing to combine both views."],["Compare SAML, OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect.","SAML is an XML-based federation protocol for web SSO using assertions. OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that issues access tokens for delegated API access; it is not an authentication protocol. OIDC adds an identity layer (ID token, a JWT) on top of OAuth 2.0 for authentication."],["How do CASB, SWG, ZTNA and SASE relate?","A CASB gives visibility and control over SaaS use; a secure web gateway filters web traffic; ZTNA gives per-application access instead of network-level VPN. SSE bundles those cloud-delivered security services, and SASE combines SSE with SD-WAN networking."],["How do tokenization and masking differ, and when would you use each?","Tokenization replaces a value with a random token and keeps the mapping in a secured vault, so authorized systems can detokenize (common for card numbers). Masking hides part or all of a value for display or produces irreversible substitute data for testing."],["What makes a design resilient rather than just redundant?","Redundancy duplicates components; resilience also removes single points of failure across power, network, region and people, fails gracefully to reduced function, recovers automatically, and is proven by testing failover, not just assumed."]],"3":[["How do TPM, HSM and secure enclave differ?","A TPM is a chip on one device that stores keys and platform measurements for measured boot and attestation. An HSM is a dedicated, tamper-resistant appliance or cloud service that generates, stores and uses keys at scale for many applications. A secure enclave (trusted execution environment) isolates code and data in use inside the CPU."],["Why is envelope encryption used in cloud KMS designs?","Data is encrypted with a data encryption key (DEK), and the DEK is encrypted with a key encryption key (KEK) held in the KMS or HSM. Bulk data never leaves the app, the master key never leaves the KMS, and rotating the KEK only requires re-wrapping DEKs."],["What is forward secrecy and how is it achieved?","Forward secrecy means a stolen long-term private key cannot decrypt past sessions. It is achieved with ephemeral Diffie-Hellman key exchange (ECDHE), so each session key is generated fresh and discarded. TLS 1.3 requires it."],["How do SPF, DKIM and DMARC work together?","SPF lists servers allowed to send for a domain. DKIM signs messages with a domain key published in DNS. DMARC tells receivers what to do when neither passes in alignment with the From domain (none, quarantine, reject) and where to send reports."],["Why are compensating controls common in OT/ICS environments?","Controllers often cannot be patched, rebooted or run agents because of availability and safety requirements and vendor certification. Teams segment them (Purdue model zones, unidirectional gateways), restrict and monitor remote access, use passive network monitoring and allow-list changes instead."],["What is post-quantum cryptography and why plan for it now?","Algorithms believed resistant to quantum attacks, such as the NIST-standardized ML-KEM for key establishment and ML-DSA for signatures. Data harvested today could be decrypted later (harvest now, decrypt later), so organizations inventory crypto, build crypto agility and plan migrations."]],"4":[["What makes SIEM alert tuning effective?","Start from a baseline of normal activity, fix parsing and normalization, suppress known-good sources with narrow and documented exceptions, add context (asset criticality, identity) to scoring, and measure true- and false-positive rates so tuning does not create blind spots."],["Explain the difference between IoCs and TTPs, and why TTPs matter more.","Indicators of compromise are artifacts such as hashes, IPs and domains that are cheap for attackers to change. TTPs describe how the adversary operates (for example credential dumping from LSASS), which is harder to change, so detections built on behavior last longer (the Pyramid of Pain)."],["How should vulnerabilities be prioritized beyond CVSS base score?","Combine severity with exploitability (EPSS probability, CISA KEV listing, public exploit), asset criticality and exposure (internet-facing, data sensitivity), compensating controls and business impact, then set SLAs by the resulting priority."],["List the incident response phases and a key action in each.","Preparation (playbooks, tooling, contacts), detection and analysis (triage, scoping), containment (isolate hosts, disable accounts), eradication (remove persistence, patch), recovery (restore, monitor closely), post-incident activity (lessons learned, control improvements)."],["What is the order of volatility and why does it matter?","Collect the most short-lived evidence first: CPU registers and cache, memory and running processes and network connections, temporary files, disk, remote logs, then archival media. Shutting down or imaging disk first destroys memory-resident evidence."],["How does hypothesis-driven threat hunting work?","The hunter forms a testable hypothesis from intelligence or ATT&CK (for example, attackers use scheduled tasks for persistence), identifies the data needed, queries for anomalies, investigates results, and turns confirmed patterns into automated detections."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true});
